<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>3.1.3: Interpreting the Slope and Vertical Intercept of a Linear Model</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00695</md:content-id>
    <md:title>3.1.3: Interpreting the Slope and Vertical Intercept of a Linear Model</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7038a58f-5a98-49dd-873c-7670d33b77fa</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>The scatter plot shows the sale price of several food items, \(y\), and the cost of the ingredients used to produce those items, \(x\), as well as a line that models the data. The line is also represented by the equation \(y = 3.48x + 0.76\).</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Scatterplot and line of best fit."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/741915e23b3675724338e3edda9489176518a7e5"/></media></figure></para>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the predicted sale price of an item that has ingredients that cost $1.50? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>$5.98 from the equation \((3.48⋅1.5+0.76=5.98)\), or approximately $6 from the scatter plot graph</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the predicted ingredient cost of an item that has a sale price of $7? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>$1.79 from the equation \(\left(\frac{7-0.76}{3.48}\;=\;1.79\right)\), or approximately $1.75 from the scatter plot graph
    </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the slope of the linear model?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>3.48.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What does the slope value mean in this situation?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. This means that for every extra dollar spent on ingredients, the sale price of the food item is expected to increase by about $3.48 </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#5-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the 𝑦-intercept of the linear model?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>0.76</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#6-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What does the value of the \(y\)-intercept mean in this situation? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. This means that a food item that is made with free ingredients would still be sold for approximately 76 cents.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#7-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Does the value of the \(y\)-intercept make sense in this situation?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
    <list>
      <item>This doesn’t seem to make sense because something that is free to make should not cost anything for the customer. </item>
      <item>This might make sense since it would still cost some money to either run the machines or pay the people to make the item. </item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

